\begin{abstract}
Why Xenopus laevis tadpoles temporarily lose tail-regeneration competence remains a question about developmental regulation and variation between animals. A potentially discriminating explanation is that refractory animals fail to couple an early wound response to a later epidermal regeneration-initiating-cell and extracellular-matrix program, rather than simply producing less injury signaling. This article frames that interaction against established RIC biology, intrinsic transition failure, metabolic gating, and tissue-composition alternatives. The available evidence does not establish a temporal association, its magnitude or uncertainty, or predictive improvement over developmental stage and known regenerative programs.

Nor does it establish novelty or provide independent biological validation. The resulting contribution is therefore an unresolved mechanistic framework, not a computational discovery or a demonstrated mechanism. Its value lies in specifying the distinction that future observations and reciprocal rescue-versus-block experiments must resolve, while preserving the difference between stage-level expression contrasts and prediction of individual regeneration outcomes.
\end{abstract}
