\section{Introduction and biological context}
The biological target is the temporary loss and subsequent return of tail-regeneration competence in Xenopus laevis, with competence varying between animals. The proposed stage-40 versus stage-46 comparison addresses a competent–refractory contrast, but does not by itself explain later recovery. A satisfactory mechanism must therefore distinguish developmental regulation from an animal-level predictor and eventually account for both loss and restoration of competence.

Transient regeneration-initiating cells, their basal-epidermal origin, and extracellular-matrix remodeling defects belong to the stated prior-knowledge boundary. The narrower question is whether developmental stage changes the relationship between an early injury response and subsequent activation of this regenerative program. Such a conditional relationship would still need to be distinguished from intrinsic failure of the epidermal transition, metabolic gating, and changes in tissue composition; its novelty cannot be assumed.

\section{Methods and evidence}
The analysis consisted of a qualitative synthesis separating supplied biological background, proposed analytical strategies, and documented evidence limitations. It compared the temporal-coupling proposal with the competing explanations specified for the study and identified which observations would be necessary to distinguish them. It did not include retrieval of GEO sample records, expression-data processing, model fitting, or a completed literature search.

The synthesis assessed the proposed inference against its stated requirements: verified biological replicates, appropriate stage and known-program baselines, protection against tissue-composition and batch confounding, and validation on independent biological material. Proposed analyses and perturbations were retained as prospective tests rather than reported as completed work. No numerical effect, uncertainty interval, predictive performance, or intervention outcome was inferred from strategy descriptions.

