\section{Discriminating validation and the unresolved outcome}
Discriminating validation would require reciprocal perturbations that alter the proposed injury-to-epidermal transition without merely changing the strength of the initial wound response. In Xenopus laevis tadpoles, the prospective comparison is a stage-46 rescue paired with a corresponding stage-40 block, with early injury signaling, later epidermal regeneration-initiating-cell (RIC) and extracellular-matrix (ECM) activation, and morphological regeneration measured separately. This separation is essential: improved regrowth alone would not identify which step had been restored, while increased epidermal activation alone would not establish recovery of regeneration. [C14, C22]

Under the coupling hypothesis, a selective stage-46 rescue should restore later epidermal activation and improve regeneration without requiring a larger early wound response. The reciprocal stage-40 intervention should reduce later activation and regeneration while preserving the early response. These are conditional predictions of the proposed framework, not observed perturbation effects; their discriminating value depends on demonstrating that the manipulation affects the relationship between the early and later responses rather than suppressing or amplifying both together. [C14, C22]

The interpretation must also distinguish restoration of coupling from bypass of the proposed defect. Directly inducing a downstream epidermal program could, in principle, improve regeneration under either a coupling defect or a failure to enter the RIC state, so such a rescue would not by itself separate those explanations. Likewise, a stage-40 block that also diminishes the initial wound response would remain compatible with inadequate injury signaling. The informative result would therefore be a reciprocal change in later epidermal activation and regrowth whose interpretation is supported by a separately measured, preserved early response and a selective intervention. [C14]

The proposed controls are integral to that interpretation. Sham and injury controls would distinguish treatment-associated changes from the response to amputation; toxicity assessment and stage matching would be needed to avoid interpreting general impairment or altered development as a regeneration-specific block. A blinded morphological endpoint should be defined at a prespecified post-amputation time, alongside the separate early-response and later-epidermal readouts.

However, intervention targets, selective manipulations, treatment windows, sample-size assumptions, and quantitative decision thresholds have not been established, so the reciprocal strategy remains a causal test outline rather than an executable reagent-level experiment. [C22, C12]

Independent biological support and predictive improvement would answer different questions. Improvement over stage-only, RIC/ECM-only, and known-signaling baselines on held-out biological units would support the usefulness of a quantitative rule, but would not by itself establish a causal mechanism. Independent support would require previously unseen biological specimens or independently generated perturbation and phenotype evidence, evaluated without using their outcomes to select the prediction. Agreement among bulk, single-cell, and spatial measurements from the same study could provide complementary evidence, but comparing those modalities or their component accessions would not constitute independent biological validation. [C25, C17]

The available evidence establishes neither tier of result: no numerical effect size, uncertainty interval, animal-level predictive performance, or independent validation outcome is documented. This absence is not a measured null effect and supplies no biological evidence against coupling failure, intrinsic transition failure, metabolic gating, or the competing confounding explanations. Novelty is a separate unresolved requirement: without a completed prior-art assessment of the interaction itself, the stage-dependent coupling rule cannot be described as previously unreported merely because it is framed as a candidate mechanism. [C20, C16, C10]

The defensible outcome is thus an unresolved mechanistic framework rather than a validated mechanism, a replicated computational association, or an established novel hypothesis. Its substantive contribution is to make the decisive contrast explicit: restoration or disruption of later epidermal activation and regeneration must be distinguished from a change in early injury-response intensity alone. The evidence therefore shifts the central argument from identifying another regeneration-associated program to establishing whether the developmental switch changes the conditional passage from injury response to regenerative activation. [C17, C14]

\section{Limitations}
\begin{itemize}
\item The biological background is supplied context rather than a completed reassessment of the cited primary literature. The temporal-coupling proposal has no established novelty assessment. Relevant claims: C29, C10, C21.
\item No verified sample manifest, biological replicate structure, expression analysis, or phenotype linkage is available. Within-animal trajectories, balanced single-cell comparisons, and individual-outcome prediction therefore remain unsupported. Relevant claims: C18, C19, C26, C27, C28.
\item No measured effect, uncertainty interval, baseline comparison, or independent biological validation is established. The absence of results must not be represented as a null biological finding. Relevant claims: C20, C24, C25, C16.
\item The proposed perturbation logic lacks identified selective interventions and quantitative decision criteria, and the initial stage contrast does not address later recovery. Relevant claims: C12, C23.
\end{itemize}
\section{Open obligations}
\begin{itemize}
\item Build a cited prior-art ledger through the investigation date and determine whether the specific stage-dependent conditional relationship has already been reported. Relevant claim: C10.
\item Retrieve and verify the GEO sample structure, assay units, pooling, biological replicates, stage–time coverage, batches, accessibility, and regeneration-outcome annotations before selecting an estimable model. Relevant claims: C18, C19, C27, C28.
\item Define early injury-response and later RIC/ECM measurements without selecting them on the validation outcomes; establish their temporal and cellular interpretation. Relevant claims: C31, C13.
\item Estimate the proposed relationship with uncertainty and compare it against stage-only, RIC/ECM-only, and known-signaling baselines using appropriate biological units and composition controls. Relevant claims: C24, C15, C20.
\item Specify a selective rescue-versus-block intervention, treatment windows, blinded endpoint, quantitative falsification criteria, and sham, injury, toxicity, and stage controls. Relevant claims: C12, C14, C22.
\item Obtain independent biological evidence from unseen specimens or conditions, rather than treating another modality from the same study as replication. Relevant claim: C25.
\item Determine whether a supported mechanism also accounts for later recovery of competence in Xenopus laevis and distinguish this from cross-species nutritional observations. Relevant claims: C23, C11.
\end{itemize}
\section{Conclusions}
Stage-dependent coupling between the early injury response and a later epidermal RIC/ECM program is a testable candidate explanation, but no temporal association, novel predictor, or causal mechanism is established.

The decisive scientific distinction is not whether known regenerative programs differ between stages, but whether an additional conditional dependency survives established-program and composition controls and receives independent biological support.

The appropriate outcome is unresolved. Further work must establish novelty, identifiability, quantitative performance, and a selective biological test before the candidate can explain either temporary loss or subsequent restoration of competence.

